{:edb/db-before-t 2413 :edb/db-after-t 2414 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535726 50 #inst "2026-10-09T06:01:05.441Z" 13194139535726 true] [17592186166597 1100 "Given $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)$, compute $P(X = 3)$." 13194139535726 false] [17592186166597 1100 "Given $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right),$ compute $P(X = 3).$" 13194139535726 true] [17592186166597 1145 "If $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}(p)$, then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n{(1 - p)^{x - 1}p,\\begin{vmatrix}x = 1, 2, 3, … \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nHere,\n\n$$\nX \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nso the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{3})^{x - 1}(\\frac{2}{3}),\\begin{vmatrix}x = 1, 2, 3, … \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X = 3) &= f(3) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1}{3})^{2}(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{27}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535726 false] [17592186166597 1145 "If $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069741/assets/5973b712a92cadd7bc301f7e8e859a1a53a551cfeee0b0ac1404ddbfc352ac56.png)\n\nHere, $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069741/assets/a7c5af2adc67390439a5d46933083b796c30fc55f0f1f5ff8cd9d374a905695d.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =f(3) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535726 true] [17592186166598 1100 "Given $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac 37\\right)$, compute $P(X < 5)$." 13194139535726 false] [17592186166598 1100 "Given $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac 37\\right),$ compute $P(X < 5).$" 13194139535726 true] [17592186166598 1145 "If $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}(p)$, then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n{(1 - p)^{x - 1}p,\\begin{vmatrix}x = 1, 2, 3, … \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nHere,\n\n$$\nX \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac 37\\right)\n$$\n\nso the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{4}{7})^{x - 1}(\\frac{3}{7}),\\begin{vmatrix}x = 1, 2, 3, … \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X < 5) &= P(X ∈ {1, 2, 3, 4}) \\\\\n&= P(X = 1) + P(X = 2) + P(X = 3) + P(X = 4) \\\\\n&= f(1) + f(2) + f(3) + f(4) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{4}{7})^{0}(\\frac{3}{7}) + (\\frac{4}{7})^{1}(\\frac{3}{7}) + (\\frac{4}{7})^{2}(\\frac{3}{7}) + (\\frac{4}{7})^{3}(\\frac{3}{7}) \\\\\n&\\approx 0.893\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $3$ decimal places." 13194139535726 false] [17592186166598 1145 "If $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069741/assets/e31c12bf515605c7cb46d96a55a8e2a3cab7eaeb5897bb04cfbe87d4d6cc6875.png)\n\nHere, $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac 37\\right),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069741/assets/5e7f6f2abab85141723e1ef5bd97157968584623433cd5a3e4d0454a39ef000b.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3,4}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3)+P(X=4) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{4}{7})}^{0}(\\frac{3}{7})+{(\\frac{4}{7})}^{1}(\\frac{3}{7})+{(\\frac{4}{7})}^{2}(\\frac{3}{7})+{(\\frac{4}{7})}^{3}(\\frac{3}{7}) \\\\  & ≈0.893\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto  $3$ decimal places." 13194139535726 true] [17592186166599 1100 "Given that $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac 15\\right)$, compute $P(2 \\leq X \\leq 3)$." 13194139535726 false] [17592186166599 1100 "Given that $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac 15\\right),$ compute $P(2 \\leq X \\leq 3).$" 13194139535726 true] [17592186166599 1145 "If $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}(p)$, then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n{(1 - p)^{x - 1}p,\\begin{vmatrix}x = 1, 2, 3, … \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nHere,\n\n$$\nX \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac 15\\right)\n$$\n\nso the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{4}{5})^{x - 1}(\\frac{1}{5}),\\begin{vmatrix}x = 1, 2, 3, … \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(2 \\le X \\le 3) &= P(X ∈ {2, 3}) \\\\\n&= P(X = 2) + P(X = 3) \\\\\n&= f(2) + f(3) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{4}{5})^{1}(\\frac{1}{5}) + (\\frac{4}{5})^{2}(\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{36}{125}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535726 false] [17592186166599 1145 "If $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069741/assets/e31c12bf515605c7cb46d96a55a8e2a3cab7eaeb5897bb04cfbe87d4d6cc6875.png)\n\nHere, $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac 15\\right),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069741/assets/cb4d54529b778e27c44ea6611a366eef6e540d9de5343e3425797f03d51c54de.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2\\le X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in {2,3}) \\\\  & =P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{1}(\\frac{1}{5})+{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}(\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{125}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535726 true] [17592186166600 1100 "Given $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac 56\\right)$, compute $P(X > 2)$." 13194139535726 false] [17592186166600 1100 "Given $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac 56\\right),$ compute $P(X > 2).$" 13194139535726 true] [17592186166600 1145 "If $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}(p)$, then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n{(1 - p)^{x - 1}p,\\begin{vmatrix}x = 1, 2, 3, … \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nHere,\n\n$$\nX \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac 56\\right)\n$$\n\nso the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{6})^{x - 1}(\\frac{5}{6}),\\begin{vmatrix}x = 1, 2, 3… \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many values of $X$ such that $X > 2$. However, we can simplify the computation by using the complement instead:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X > 2) &= 1 - P(X \\le 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le 2) &= P(X ∈ {1, 2}) \\\\\n&= P(X = 1) + P(X = 2) \\\\\n&= f(1) + f(2) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1}{6})^{0}(\\frac{5}{6}) + (\\frac{1}{6})^{1}(\\frac{5}{6}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{35}{36}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X > 2) &= 1 - P(X \\le 2) \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{35}{36} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{36}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535726 false] [17592186166600 1145 "If $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069741/assets/ab3f034397a81b2369008754aef3c05f998f180b5127e543a05ab408c743cf8a.png)\n\nHere, $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac 56\\right),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069741/assets/efc7a45d9b19867340454ad32abe129214dcca021394f259f79d1e26643da5c7.png)\n\nThere are infinitely many values of $X$ such that $X > 2.$ However, we can simplify the computation by using the complement instead:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>2) & =1-P(X\\le 2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =P(X\\in {1,2}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{6})}^{0}(\\frac{5}{6})+{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{1}(\\frac{5}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>2) & =1-P(X\\le 2) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{35}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535726 true] [17592186166759 1020 #edb/ref 17592186363317 13194139535726 true] [17592186166759 1020 #edb/ref 17592186363324 13194139535726